<article class="env-definition" id="003T">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/003T">Definition <span data-tag="003T">4.35.1</span>.</a> Let $p : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{C}$ be a functor. We say that $\mathcal{S}$ is <em>fibred in groupoids</em> over $\mathcal{C}$ if the following two conditions hold: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
For every morphism $f : V \to U$ in $\mathcal{C}$ and every lift $x$ of $U$ there is a lift $\phi : y \to x$ of $f$ with target $x$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every pair of morphisms $\phi : y \to x$ and $ \psi : z \to x$ and any morphism $f : p(z) \to p(y)$ such that $p(\phi ) \circ f = p(\psi )$ there exists a unique lift $\chi : z \to y$ of $f$ such that $\phi \circ \chi = \psi $. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>